  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>geometric sequence q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A school has a fish pond. Each month, the number of fish in the pond is multiplied by the same number, so the monthly numbers form a geometric progression (GP). In month {@p@}, there are {@tp@} fish. In month {@q@}, there are {@tq@} fish.</p>

<p>(a) Find the common ratio, \(r\).</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b) Find the number of fish in month 1, which is the first term, \(a\).</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c) Write an expression for the \(n^{th}\) term. Use \(n\) in your answer.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>

<p>(d) Find the number of fish in month {@m@}.</p>
<p>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p>(e) In which month are there {@target@} fish?</p>
<p>[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Month {@p@} has {@tp@} fish and month {@q@} has {@tq@} fish. The numbers form a geometric progression (GP).</p>

<p>(a) <strong>Common ratio:</strong></p>
<p>Use the term formula \( T_n = ar^{n-1} \) for the two months given.</p>
<p>Month {@p@}:</p>
<p>\[ ar^{ {@p-1@} } = {@tp@} \qquad \text{(equation 1)} \]</p>
<p>Month {@q@}:</p>
<p>\[ ar^{ {@q-1@} } = {@tq@} \qquad \text{(equation 2)} \]</p>
<p>Divide equation 2 by equation 1. This eliminates \(a\):</p>
<p>\[ \frac{ar^{ {@q-1@} }}{ar^{ {@p-1@} }} = \frac{ {@tq@} }{ {@tp@} } \]</p>
<p>\[ r^{ {@gap@} } = {@quot@} \]</p>
<p>Find the number that, multiplied {@gap@} times, gives {@quot@}:</p>
<p>\[ {@r@}^{ {@gap@} } = {@quot@} \]</p>
<p>So \( r = {@ta1@} \).</p>

<p>(b) <strong>First term:</strong></p>
<p>Put \( r = {@r@} \) into equation 1:</p>
<p>\[ a \times {@r@}^{ {@p-1@} } = {@tp@} \]</p>
<p>\[ a \times {@r^(p-1)@} = {@tp@} \]</p>
<p>\[ a = \frac{ {@tp@} }{ {@r^(p-1)@} } = {@ta2@} \]</p>
<p>In month 1, there are {@ta2@} fish.</p>

<p>(c) <strong>The \(n^{th}\) term:</strong></p>
<p>Put \( a = {@a@} \) and \( r = {@r@} \) into \( T_n = ar^{n-1} \):</p>
<p>\[ T_n = {@a@} \times {@r@}^{n-1} \]</p>

<p>(d) <strong>Month {@m@}:</strong></p>
<p>\[ T_{ {@m@} } = {@a@} \times {@r@}^{ {@m@} - 1 } = {@a@} \times {@r@}^{ {@m-1@} } = {@a@} \times {@r^(m-1)@} = {@ta4@} \]</p>
<p>In month {@m@}, there are {@ta4@} fish.</p>

<p>(e) <strong>Which month has {@target@} fish?</strong></p>
<p>\[ {@a@} \times {@r@}^{n-1} = {@target@} \]</p>
<p>Divide both sides by {@a@}:</p>
<p>\[ {@r@}^{n-1} = {@target/a@} \]</p>
<p>Write {@target/a@} as a power of {@r@}:</p>
<p>\[ {@r@}^{ {@k-1@} } = {@r^(k-1)@} \]</p>
<p>So \( n - 1 = {@k-1@} \), which gives \( n = {@ta5@} \).</p>
<p>There are {@target@} fish in month {@ta5@}.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
